export default "<page title=\"Home\" url=\"/index.html\">\nFor Class 11 & 12 students and parents\n\n## Choosing a college should not be a guess.\nMost families decide on a cousin's advice and a coaching-class rumour. We sit with your child, look at their marks, interests and your budget, then go and find the colleges that actually fit — in India or abroad.\nGet free guidance How it works\n\n10% of Indian students ever get proper career guidance\n1:3,000 counsellor-to-student ratio in India. Global norm is 1:250\n₹0 what school counselling and India admissions cost you\n\n## Start with a free consultation\nFill this in and we continue on WhatsApp. About a minute.\n\n### Free for India and schools\nNo fee for school counselling or India admissions.\n\n### Parents in the room\nWe speak to the parent, not just the student.\n\n### India and abroad\nJEE, CUET, NEET and REAP — or the UK to Canada.\n\n### Through to admission\nWe stay until your child has a seat.\n\nTwo tracks\n\n## The India decision and the abroad decision are not the same decision\nDifferent calendars, different exams, very different money. We handle both — and we will tell you plainly if one of them is wrong for your family.\n\n### Studying in India Apr – Jul\n- Stream and course fit before you chase a college name\n- REAP, JEE Main, CUET, NEET — which actually apply to your child\n- State counselling rounds versus direct and management-quota seats\n- Real fee bands, hostel costs and the total four-year number\n- Recognised distance and online degrees — and how to spot fake ones How we work on India admissions →\n\n### Studying abroad 12–18 months ahead\n- Whether the return on cost genuinely works for your family\n- Country shortlist — UK, Germany, Ireland, Australia, Canada, US\n- IELTS or TOEFL timing, and the score the course really needs\n- Intakes, deadlines, SOP, scholarships and proof of funds\n- Funded routes too — assistantships and tuition-free public universities How we work on overseas admissions →\n\nWhy it is free\n\n## Somebody pays for this. It is not you.\nColleges pay us a referral fee when a student we have counselled chooses to enrol with them. That is how this works, and we would rather you heard it from us on day one than found out later.\nIt also means we say this out loud: if the right college for your child is one that pays us nothing, we will tell you. A family that recommends us to three other families is worth far more than one fee.\n\nWe never ask students or parents for money — not for the session, not for the assessment, not for the report.\nFunded study-abroad support is a paid service, priced up front. Everything else is free.\n\nHow it works\n\n## Three conversations, not a sales call\n\n1\n\n### Assessment\nA proper sitting with the student — interests, subjects they actually enjoy, marks, and what the family can spend. Parents in the room.\n\n2\n\n### Written shortlist\nA report you keep: courses that fit, colleges across safe, likely and stretch, with fees, requirements and dates set out.\n\n3\n\n### Through to admission\nForms, deadlines, counselling rounds, documents. We stay with you until your child has a seat, not until you have a PDF.\n\nAre you a school?\n\n### CBSE now requires a career counsellor for every 500 students\nThe affiliation bye-laws were amended in January 2026 for Classes 9 to 12. We run structured career sessions in Jaipur schools at no cost to the school. What a school session looks like →\n\nQuestions parents ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Is this really free, or is there a fee later?\nSchool counselling and India admissions are free throughout. No registration fee, no assessment fee, no report fee, no fee at admission. Funded study-abroad support is the one paid service, and its fee is agreed in writing before any work starts.\n\n### Will you push my child towards a particular college?\nYou will get a shortlist with reasons attached, including options that pay us nothing. Ask which colleges we have an arrangement with and we will tell you.\n\n### My child has not chosen a stream yet. Too early?\nClass 11 is the right time, not too early. Stream selection closes more doors than any other decision, and it is usually made on the least information.\n\n### What happens to our details?\nThey stay with our counselling team, held with signed parental consent. Nothing is shared with any institution unless you ask us to apply, and you can have everything deleted at any time.\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Studying in India\" url=\"/india.html\">\nTrack one\n\n## Getting a seat in India is a calendar problem before it is a marks problem\nFamilies lose good options every year by finding out about a deadline two weeks after it closed. Here is how the year actually runs, and where we come in.\n\nThe year\n\n## What happens when\nDates shift a little each year. The shape does not.\n\n1\n\n### Nov – Jan\nEntrance registrations open. JEE Main session one, CUET notification, NEET application. This is the window students miss most often.\n\n2\n\n### Feb – Apr\nBoard exams, then entrance exams. Nothing can be fixed here — the preparation either happened or it did not.\n\n3\n\n### May – Aug\nResults, then counselling. REAP rounds, state and central counselling, direct admissions. Decisions get made in days, not weeks.\n\nThe part nobody explains\n\n## Two different doors into the same college\n\n### State counselling\nIn Rajasthan, REAP allots most engineering seats in affiliated private colleges by rank, through a central portal. You register, fill choices, and get allotted. No college application, no negotiation.\n- Decided purely by rank and choice order\n- Choice filling is where families make expensive mistakes\n- Later rounds open seats that looked gone in round one\n\n### Direct and management quota\nPrivate universities outside the state process admit directly, and affiliated colleges hold a management quota. Higher fees, different timing, and far more variation in what you actually get.\n- Ask what the fee covers before anyone asks for a deposit\n- Verify recognition — UGC, AICTE, and the specific programme\n- Never pay a deposit to an intermediary, only to the institution\n\nWhat we do\n\n## Where a counsellor actually changes the outcome\n\n### Before the exams\n- Which entrances are worth registering for, given the child's real position\n- Stream and course fit — the decision that closes the most doors later\n- A budget conversation with the parents, early and honestly\n\n### After the results\n- Choice filling — ordering, not just picking\n- Shortlist across safe, likely and stretch, with real fee totals\n- Documents, deadlines and counselling rounds, tracked with you\n\nDistance and online degrees\n\n## A recognised distance degree can be a sensible choice. An unrecognised one is worth nothing.\nSome students need to work, support their family, prepare for a competitive exam, or simply cannot move city. A recognised distance or online degree lets them keep studying without a full-time campus place. Under UGC's 2020 regulations, recognised undergraduate and postgraduate degrees earned this way are treated as equivalent to the same degrees earned on campus.\n\n### When it can make sense\n- The student needs to earn, or the family cannot cover hostel and living costs in another city\n- The student is preparing full-time for a competitive exam and wants a degree alongside\n- The subject is one distance study allows — arts, commerce, management or computer applications, for example\n- Options include IGNOU nationally, Rajasthan's state open university (VMOU, Kota), and online programmes from universities UGC has approved for online mode\n\n### What it cannot be used for\nUGC does not allow these subjects, or their allied fields, to be taught by distance or online mode:\n- Engineering, Architecture and Law\n- Medical, Dental, Nursing, Pharmacy, Physiotherapy, Occupational Therapy and other paramedical fields\n- Agriculture and Horticulture\n- Hotel Management, Catering Technology, Culinary Sciences and Aircraft Maintenance\n- Visual Arts and Sports\nMPhil and PhD programmes cannot be taken this way either. Anyone selling a distance degree in these subjects is selling something UGC does not recognise.\n\nBefore you pay any fee\n\n### Four checks that take ten minutes\n- Recognition, for your session.Check on the UGC Distance Education Bureau website (deb.ugc.ac.in) that the university is approved for distance or online mode for the year you are joining, and for that programme. Approval is given session by session.\n- Not on the fake list.UGC publishes a list of fake universities on ugc.gov.in. Some have official-sounding names.\n- The right territory.For distance (ODL) programmes, a state university may only operate within its own state. A study centre outside that area is a warning sign.\n- No franchising.Universities may not run distance programmes through franchise partners. If an agent or “partner centre” is running the course on a university's behalf, ask the university directly.\nSources: UGC Distance Education Bureau — FAQ· UGC — list of fake universities. Last checked 30 September 2026.\n\nFree, and here is why\n\n## Colleges pay us. You never do.\nIf a college on your shortlist pays us nothing, we will still put it there and tell you why. Ask us at any point which colleges we have an arrangement with and we will answer — how we are paidis set out in full.\nThe right college for your child is a separate question from who pays us. We keep it that way.\n\n## Start with a free consultation\nTell us where your child is and we will take it from there. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Studying abroad\" url=\"/abroad.html\">\nTrack two\n\n## Going abroad is a money decision wearing an education costume\nThe course matters. So does the country, the loan, the work rights afterwards and what the degree is actually worth when your child comes home. We work through all of it, including the parts that argue against going.\n\nThe question nobody asks\n\n## Some universities charge you ₹40 lakh. Some pay you to attend.\nMost study-abroad consultants are paid a commission by the universities they send students to. That is not a scandal — it is just how the industry works. But it has a consequence almost nobody says out loud: the universities that pay commission are the ones charging full fees. The funded routes pay agents nothing, so nobody shows them to you.\nWe look at both. For a strong student in the right field, a funded place can turn a forty-lakh decision into a four-lakh one.\n\n### Assistantships in the US and Canada\nA graduate assistantship is a paid teaching or research role attached to your admission. At most universities it waives tuition entirely, pays a monthly stipend, and subsidises health insurance. International students are eligible. Appointments typically run 10–20 hours a week, and in the US, federal rules cap international students at 20 hours during term.\n\n### Tuition-free public universities in Europe\nMost public universities in Germany charge no tuition at all to international students — only a semester contribution, which DAAD puts at €70–430. There are real exceptions: Baden-Württemberg charges non-EU students €1,500 per semester, and TUM in Bavaria charges €4,000–6,000 per semester for master's programmes. The difference between picking the right state and the wrong one is several lakh rupees a year.\n\nBeing straight with you\n\n### This is not available to everyone, and we will tell you if it is not\nFunded places are competitive and concentrated — research degrees more than taught ones, and some fields far more than others. Your marks, your research background and the strength of your written application all decide it. A student who is not a realistic candidate should hear that from us early, while there is still time to plan something sensible, rather than after four months of hope and application fees.\n\nHow this is paid for\n\n## Our India work is free. This part is not, and here is why.\nSchool counselling and Indian college admissions cost you nothing, because colleges pay us a referral fee when a student enrols. We have said that on every page since the day we started.\nFunded study abroad does not work that way. The universities that waive your tuition and pay you a stipend do not pay agents. So there is no commission to collect, and anyone offering to guide you to them \"for free\" is either not doing the work or is quietly steering you somewhere that does pay them.\nSo we charge for it directly, and we publish what we know for free. Read the guides, do it yourself, and never speak to us — that is a completely legitimate outcome and the guides are written to make it possible.\n\nFree\nGuides on funded routes, starting with US assistantshipsand tuition-free Germany, with more being added. Published openly. No email address required to read them.\n\nPaid\nOne-to-one application support: shortlisting against your actual profile, faculty outreach strategy, SOP and research statement work, deadline management through to the visa file.\nFee agreed up front in writing before any work starts.\n\nStart here\n\n## Four questions before you look at a single university\n\n01\n\n### What is the total number?\nNot tuition. Tuition plus living costs plus travel plus visa plus insurance, times the length of the course, at a realistic exchange rate. Write it down before you fall in love with a campus.\n02\n\n### How is it being paid?\nSavings, education loan, or both. If it is a loan, the repayment starts whether or not the job does. We will run that maths with you rather than around you.\n03\n\n### What happens after the degree?\nPost-study work rights differ sharply by country and change with politics. A two-year work window and a six-month one are completely different investments.\n04\n\n### Would the same money go further in India?\nSometimes yes. We will say so. A good Indian programme plus the saved capital beats a weak foreign one more often than anyone in this industry admits.\n\nThe runway\n\n## Twelve to eighteen months, worked backwards\nAlmost every abroad application that goes wrong went wrong on timing, not on merit.\n\n1\n\n### 18–12 months out\nCountry and course shortlist. Budget agreed with the family. IELTS or TOEFL booked with room to retake.\n\n2\n\n### 12–6 months out\nApplications, SOP drafted properly rather than downloaded, LORs requested early, scholarship deadlines mapped.\n\n3\n\n### 6–0 months out\nOffers, loan sanction, proof of funds, visa file, accommodation. This stage is paperwork discipline, nothing else.\n\nWhere we help\n\n## What we do and what we do not\n\n### We do\n- Country and course shortlisting against your actual budget\n- Test planning — which test, what score, booked when\n- SOP and LOR structure, reviewed line by line\n- Scholarship and education loan groundwork\n- Deadline tracking through to the visa file\n\n### We do not\n- Guarantee an admission or a visa. Nobody honest can\n- Write the SOP for your child. Admissions officers can tell\n- Push a country because it is easy to place students in\n- Charge for anything before the fee is agreed in writing\n\nQuestions parents ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Is an agent the same as a counsellor?\nNo. An agent is paid to place students with particular universities. A counsellor should be working out what is right for your child first. Where an institution pays us, we say so openly and will name it if you ask. Funded routes pay nobody, so for those we charge you directly and agree the fee first.\n\n### My child's marks are average. Is abroad still possible?\nOften yes — many good foreign universities weigh the application as a whole rather than a single percentage. Whether it is worth the money is the harder question, and that is the one we will actually discuss with you.\n\n### How early should we start?\nClass 11 is not too early for the money conversation. For applications, twelve to eighteen months before the intake. Families who start six months out end up choosing from what is left.\n\n### What about education loans?\nWe will help you understand collateral requirements, moratorium periods and what banks look for, and refer you to lenders. We do not take commission from any lender.\n\n## Talk it through before you spend anything\nThe first conversation is free, and sometimes it ends with us telling you not to go. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"For schools\" url=\"/schools.html\">\nFor principals and coordinators\n\n## CBSE now requires a career counsellor for every 500 students\nThe affiliation bye-laws were amended in January 2026, covering Classes 9 to 12. Most schools are still working out how to meet it. We run structured career sessions in Jaipur schools, at no cost to the school.\n\nWhat changed\n\n## Clause 2.4.12, amended January 2026\nThe requirement came out of a public interest litigation in the Rajasthan High Court on academic stress and the absence of structured career guidance.\n\n01\n\n### One per 500\nA career counsellor for every 500 students across Classes 9 to 12.\n02\n\n### Qualification\nA graduate or postgraduate degree in Humanities, Science, Social Sciences, Management, Education or Technology.\n03\n\n### Hub and spoke\nLarger schools may mentor nearby schools, so resources can be shared rather than duplicated.\nWe are not a substitute for reading the circular. If you are assessing how your school meets the requirement, confirm the position directly with the CBSE Regional Office, Ajmer — and we are happy to share what we have understood from it.\n\nThe session\n\n## What we actually do in a school\n\n### Group session — 45 minutes\n- How stream and course choices shape the next ten years\n- Entrance examsthat apply to this cohort, plainly explained\n- What colleges actually cost, all in, over the full course\n- Questions, openly — usually the most useful part\n\n### Individual assessment\n- A written assessment form, filled with parental consent\n- Interests, strengths, marks and family budget\n- A personal report for each student who wants one\n- Follow-up with the family after board results\n\nTerms, stated plainly\n\n## No cost to the school. No selling in your classroom.\nWe do not charge the school and we do not charge your students or their parents. We are paid by colleges when a student we have counselled later chooses to enrol with them ( how we are paid) — and that conversation happens with the family, after board results, away from your campus.\nNo commercial activity on school premises, no fees collected on site, and nothing shared with any institution without written parental consent.\nYour students get guidance. Your school gets a record of it. Nothing is sold in your building.\n\nPracticalities\n\n## What we need from you\n\n1\n\n### A slot\nForty-five minutes with a class or a year group, between July and November. We work around your calendar, not the other way round.\n\n2\n\n### A room\nAny hall or classroom. We bring the forms and materials.\n\n3\n\n### A note home\nParents told in advance, and invited. Parental consent on the assessment form is signed, not assumed.\n\nQuestions schools ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Does an external counsellor satisfy the CBSE requirement?\nThat is a question for CBSE, not for us, and we will not tell you it does. The circular reads as an instruction to appoint. What we can offer is structured, documented career guidance for your students while you work out your own compliance position.\n\n### Will you collect money from our students?\nNo. Not on your premises, not afterwards, not at any stage. If anyone representing us asks a parent for money, tell us and we will end it.\n\n### What happens to student data?\nAssessment forms are held by our counselling team with signed parental consent. Nothing goes to any college unless the family asks us to apply. Any parent can ask us to delete their child's record and we will.\n\n### Can we see the material first?\nYes. Ask and we will send the session outline and a blank assessment form before you commit to anything.\n\n## Arrange a session\nMessage us and we will send the outline and a blank form before anything is agreed. Message us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"About Campuscout\" url=\"/about.html\">\nAbout\n\n## A counsellor, not a call centre\nCampuscout is a career guidance practice in Jaipur. We work with Class 11 and 12 students and their parents on college decisions — in India and abroad.\n\nWhy this exists\n\n## The numbers are not subtle\n- Around one in ten Indian students ever receives professional career guidance\n- The counsellor-to-student ratio in India is roughly 1:3,000. The global norm is 1:250\n- Most families make a ten to twenty lakh rupee decision on hearsay\nA decision this size deserves an hour of someone's undivided attention. That is the whole idea.\n\nWho you will be speaking to\n\n### A dedicated career counsellor\nEvery family works with one experienced counsellor who knows Classes 9 to 12 career choices inside out, from streams and entrance exams to colleges and courses. Sessions began after CBSE amended its affiliation bye-laws in January 2026 to require a career counsellor in every affiliated school.\nThe same person runs your sessions, writes your report and follows up afterwards, so you never have to explain your child's situation twice.\n\nHow we are paid\n\n## Colleges pay us. For India admissions, families never do.\nWhen a student we have counselled chooses to enrol at a college we work with, that college pays us a referral fee. That is how our school and India work is paid for. The one exception is funded study-abroad support, which colleges do not pay us for, so we charge for it directly and say so before we start.\nWe tell every family this at the first meeting, because the alternative is that they find out later and reasonably wonder what else we did not mention. Ask us which colleges we have arrangements with and we will tell you.\nIt also means shortlists include colleges that pay us nothing, because a family that recommends us to three other families is worth far more than one fee.\nIf the right college for your child pays us nothing, we will still tell you it is the right college.\n\nWhat we will not do\n\n## Four promises, and you can hold us to them\n\n01\n\n### We will not charge for India admissions\nNo registration fee, no assessment fee, no report fee, no fee at admission. Funded study-abroad support is the only paid service, and its fee is agreed in writing first. If anyone claiming to represent us asks for money otherwise, tell us.\n02\n\n### We will not guarantee a seat\nNobody honest can. Anyone promising a guaranteed admission for a payment is describing something you should walk away from.\n03\n\n### We will not pass on your details\nNothing goes to any college unless you ask us to apply. Ask us to delete your record and we will, at no cost.\n04\n\n### We will not hide the conflict\nYou will hear how we are paid in the first conversation, not the last. Every time.\n\nQuestions parents ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Is this really free, or is there a fee later?\nSchool counselling and India admissions are free throughout. No registration fee, no assessment fee, no report fee, no fee at admission. Funded study-abroad support is the one paid service, and its fee is agreed in writing before any work starts.\n\n### Will you push my child towards a particular college?\nYou will get a shortlist with reasons attached, including options that pay us nothing. Ask which colleges we have an arrangement with and we will tell you.\n\n### My child has not chosen a stream yet. Too early?\nClass 11 is the right time, not too early. Stream selection closes more doors than any other decision, and it is usually made on the least information.\n\n### What happens to our details?\nThey stay with our counselling team, held with signed parental consent. Nothing is shared with any institution unless you ask us to apply, and you can have everything deleted at any time.\n\n### Do you work with schools?\nYes. Principals and coordinators can read what a school session involves on the For schoolspage.\n\n## Start with a free consultation\nOne conversation, no cost, no obligation. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Free guides\" url=\"/guides.html\">\nFree guides\n\n## Everything we know about funded study abroad, published openly\nRead them, do it yourself, and never speak to us — that is a completely legitimate outcome. No email address required.\n\n### Graduate assistantships in the US US\nWhat a TA or RA covers, who realistically gets one, the application timeline, and the F-1 rules that changed in 2026. Read the guide →\n\n### Tuition-free study in Germany Germany\nWhat “free” really costs, where tuition does apply, the Class 12 rule, APS, the blocked account and work rules. Read the guide →\nMore guides are being written. Each one lists its sources and the date it was last checked.\n\n## Prefer to talk it through?\nThe first conversation is free. One-to-one support for funded applications is a paid service, with the fee agreed in writing before any work starts. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Guide: US graduate assistantships\" url=\"/guide-us-assistantships.html\">\nFree guide · Study in the US\n\n## Getting paid to study in the US: graduate assistantships\nAn assistantship is a job at the university that comes attached to your admission. A good one covers tuition and pays you a stipend. Here is how they work, who realistically gets them, and the rules you will live under.\nLast checked 30 September 2026\n\nThe basics\n\n## Three kinds of assistantship\nNames vary between universities, but almost every assistantship is one of these.\n\n01\n\n### Teaching assistantship (TA)\nYou help teach undergraduate courses — running tutorials or labs, grading, holding office hours. Funded by the department.\n02\n\n### Research assistantship (RA)\nYou work on a professor's research project, usually paid from that professor's grant. Often the work becomes part of your own thesis.\n03\n\n### Graduate assistantship (GA)\nAdministrative or support work elsewhere in the university — an office, a library, student services. Less common for international students at admission.\n\nWhat a package usually includes\n\n## Read the offer letter line by line\nA typical full assistantship includes the items below. None of them is guaranteed — each university and each offer is different, so check what yours actually says.\n- A tuition waiver — full or partial\n- A monthly stipend, paid as a salary for the work\n- Help with health insurance — often a subsidy, sometimes full cover\n- An expected workload, commonly 10–20 hours a week\nWe do not quote stipend figures here: they vary too much by university, field and city to be useful as a single number. Compare offers against the local cost of living, not against each other.\n\nBeing straight with you\n\n## Who realistically gets one\n\n### More likely\n- PhD applicants — many US PhD programmes fund every student they admit\n- Research-heavy fields, especially science, engineering and technology\n- Applicants with research experience, a strong statement of purpose and good references\n- Applicants who have identified professors whose work matches theirs\n\n### Less likely\n- Master's applicants — many taught master's programmes offer little or no funding at admission\n- Fields with few research grants\n- Applications that read as generic rather than written for that department\n- Applicants who apply after the funding deadline, which is often earlier than the admission deadline\nSome master's students find a TA or on-campus job after their first semester rather than at admission. That can help, but it should not be the plan you borrow against.\n\nThe timeline\n\n## Working backwards from an autumn start\nExact dates are set by each department. This is the usual shape.\n\n1\n\n### 12–15 months before\nShortlist programmes and professors. Check each department's funding page — look for a clear statement on whether admitted students are funded.\n\n2\n\n### December – January\nApplications for funded consideration. Many departments only consider applicants who meet an early deadline for funding.\n\n3\n\n### February – April\nOffers arrive. Under the Council of Graduate Schools April 15 Resolution, students are not obliged to accept an offer of financial support before 15 April at participating universities.\n\nThe rules\n\n## What your F-1 student status allows\n\n### While you study\n- On-campus work, including an assistantship, is limited to 20 hours a week while school is in session\n- Full-time on-campus work is allowed during official breaks\n- The university's funding letter is part of the proof of funds for your I-20, the form you need for the visa\n\n### Changes from 15 September 2026\n- A DHS rule published on 17 July 2026 replaces open-ended “duration of status” with a fixed admission period — up to the length of your programme, and no more than four years\n- Staying longer, for example for a longer PhD, means applying to USCIS for an extension\n- The grace period after your programme or OPT ends is cut from 60 days to 30\n\nAfter the degree\n\n### Optional Practical Training (OPT)\nGraduates can work in the US on OPT for 12 months. Graduates in approved STEM fields can apply for a further 24-month STEM OPT extension. Both survived the July 2026 rule, but DHS has said it is reviewing OPT, so treat future availability as a risk to plan around rather than a certainty.\n\nQuestions families ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Is an assistantship the same as a scholarship?\nNo. A scholarship or fellowship is money with no work attached. An assistantship is employment — you are paid for teaching or research work, and the tuition waiver usually comes with the job.\n\n### Can I get one for a master's degree?\nSometimes, but far less often than for a PhD. If funding is essential for your family, look hard at PhD programmes and at master's programmes that state clearly that they fund students.\n\n### Do I need to contact professors before applying?\nFor research assistantships it often helps, because RA funding comes from a professor's grant. Keep it short and specific to their work. Some departments ask applicants not to — check the department's guidance first.\n\n### Does an assistantship cover everything?\nRarely. Fees that are not tuition, the visa, flights, a deposit on housing and the first month before the stipend arrives usually fall on you. Budget for them separately.\n\nSources\n\n### Where these facts come from\nLast checked 30 September 2026. Rules and fees change, sometimes mid-year. Before you act on anything here, confirm it with the university or the official source below.\n- Federal Register — Fixed time period of admission for F and J nonimmigrants (17 July 2026)\n- Study in the States (DHS) — STEM OPT extension\n- Study in the States (DHS) — STEM OPT Hub\n- Council of Graduate Schools — April 15 Resolution FAQ\n\n## Want help with a funded application?\nOur guidesare free to use on your own. One-to-one support for funded applications is a paid service, with the fee agreed in writing before any work starts. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Guide: tuition-free study in Germany\" url=\"/guide-germany.html\">\nFree guide · Study in Germany\n\n## Studying in Germany without tuition fees — and where that stops being true\nMost public universities in Germany charge international students no tuition. But there are real costs, real exceptions, and one rule about Class 12 certificates that catches out a lot of Indian families.\nLast checked 30 September 2026\n\nRead this first\n\n### An Indian Class 12 certificate alone does not get you into a German bachelor's degree\nAccording to DAAD India, students with only a Class 12 certificate cannot be admitted directly. The routes in are:\n- A valid JEE Advanced result, for a subject-specific application\n- One completed year of a bachelor's degree in India, for subject-specific admission\n- Studienkolleg — a foundation year in Germany ending in an exam called the Feststellungsprüfung. It is taught in German, and DAAD India puts the level needed at about B1\nFor master's degrees this does not apply — an Indian bachelor's degree is the usual entry route.\n\nWhat “free” means\n\n## No tuition is not the same as no cost\n\n01\n\n### Semester contribution\nEvery student pays this each semester. It covers student services and often a local transport ticket. DAAD puts it at €70–430 depending on the university.\n02\n\n### Living costs\nDAAD estimates €900–1,200 a month for most students, depending on the city. Munich and other large cities sit at the top of that range or above it.\n03\n\n### Money you must show\nFor the student visa you must prove €992 a month — €11,904 for a year — usually in a blocked account. Providers charge set-up and monthly fees on top.\n\nWhere you will pay tuition\n\n## The exceptions, and why the state you pick matters\n\n### Baden-Württemberg\nThe whole state charges non-EU students tuition at public universities.\n- €1,500 per semester for students from outside the EU/EEA\n- Covers universities such as Heidelberg, Stuttgart, Freiburg, Mannheim and Karlsruhe\n- Doctoral students are exempt, as are some exchange students\n\n### TUM, Bavaria\nThe Technical University of Munich charges non-EU students tuition. Bavarian law lets universities set their own fees.\n- Bachelor's: usually €2,000 or €3,000 per semester\n- Master's: usually €4,000 or €6,000 per semester\n- Applies to non-EU students enrolling from winter semester 2024/25\n- A few programmes are exempt — check the programme page\nPrivate universities in Germany charge full tuition everywhere. They are a different decision and should be compared on cost like any other paid option.\n\nSteps for Indian applicants\n\n## The paperwork, in order\n\n1\n\n### APS certificate\nMandatory for Indian applicants to degree courses in Germany, and needed before the visa. The fee is ₹18,000, non-refundable. Allow 3–4 weeks, and longer in peak season.\n\n2\n\n### Applications\nDAAD India suggests researching in October–November, starting APS in January–March, and applying in March–June for a winter-semester start. Each programme sets its own deadline.\n\n3\n\n### Visa and blocked account\nAdmission letter, blocked account, health insurance and APS certificate go into the visa application.\n\nWork and staying on\n\n## What the rules allow\n\n### While you study\n- Up to 140 full days or 280 half days of work a year\n- Student assistant jobs at your own university may be counted differently — ask the university's international office\n- Part-time work helps with living costs but is not enough to fund a degree on its own\n\n### After you graduate\n- An 18-month residence permit to look for work\n- You can work in any job during those 18 months\n- It cannot be extended — you need a qualifying job to move on to a work permit or EU Blue Card\n\nQuestions families ask\n\n## Straight answers\n\n### Are courses taught in English?\nSome. DAAD India counts around 300 English-taught courses against around 2,000 in German. English-taught options are far more common at master's level. For a bachelor's, plan on learning German.\n\n### Is Germany cheaper than the UK or the US?\nOn tuition, usually by a wide margin. On living costs, less so — Munich is not cheap. Add up the semester contributions, blocked account and living costs before you decide.\n\n### Does the blocked account count as a cost?\nIt is your money, released to you monthly to live on, so it is not a fee. But it has to be in place before the visa, which is a cash-flow problem for many families. The provider's fees are a real cost.\n\n### Can I study in a tuition-free state and avoid all fees?\nYou will still pay the semester contribution, and your costs depend heavily on the city. Outside Baden-Württemberg and the Bavarian universities that charge, most public universities charge non-EU students no tuition.\n\nSources\n\n### Where these facts come from\nLast checked 30 September 2026. Rules and fees change, sometimes mid-year. Before you act on anything here, confirm it with the university or the official source below.\n- DAAD — Costs of education and living\n- DAAD India — Bachelor studies\n- Baden-Württemberg Ministry of Science — Tuition fees for international students\n- TUM — Tuition fees for students from non-EU countries\n- APS India — Academic Evaluation Centre\n\n## Want help with a Germany application?\nOur guidesare free to use on your own. One-to-one application support is a paid service, with the fee agreed in writing before any work starts. Talk to us on WhatsApp\n</page>\n\n<page title=\"Careers at Campuscout\" url=\"/careers.html\">\nCareers\n\n## Help families make one of the biggest decisions they will make\nCampuscout is a small career guidance practice in Jaipur. If you care about giving students honest advice — including advice that costs us money — we would like to hear from you.\n\nThe work\n\n## What the job actually involves\nEverything on this website is a promise someone on the team has to keep. This is what that looks like day to day.\n\n01\n\n### In schools\nRunning structured career sessionsfor Classes 9 to 12, explaining streams, entrance exams and real college costs plainly, and answering the questions students are usually too shy to ask.\n02\n\n### With families\nSitting with a student and their parents, understanding marks, interests and budget, and writing a shortlist they can keep — across safe, likely and stretch options.\n03\n\n### Through to admission\nTracking deadlines, counselling rounds and documents with each family until their child has a seat, not until they have a report.\n04\n\n### Keeping it accurate\nChecking fees, exam dates and admission rules against official sources, because families act on what we tell them.\n\nWhat matters to us\n\n## The way we work is not negotiable\n- You tell every family how we are paid, in the first conversation\n- You never take money from a student or parent for India or school work\n- You put a college on a shortlist because it fits, whether or not it pays us\n- Nothing is shared with any institution without written parental consent\n- You say “I do not know, let me check” rather than guess\nIf the right college for a child pays us nothing, you will still tell the family it is the right college.\n\nOpenings\n\n## We are hiring a Management Intern\nNow hiring\n\n### Management Intern\nTo apply, email your CV and a few lines on why Campuscout to hr@campuscout.orgwith “Management Intern” in the subject line. You can also use the form on this page and choose “Management internship”.\nNot the right role for you? We still want to hear from good people. Tell us about yourself and we will be in touch when there is a role that fits.\nFor counselling roles, a graduate or postgraduate degree in Humanities, Science, Social Sciences, Management, Education or Technology is a good starting point — it is what CBSE asks of school career counsellors. Experience with students, schools or admissions helps. Fluency in Hindi and English matters more than polish.\nWe use what you send only to consider you for a role at Campuscout. Ask us to delete it at any time and we will.\n\n## Tell us about yourself\n</page>";
